## **Mutable vs Immutable Strings**
| **Feature** | **String (Immutable)** | **StringBuilder / StringBuffer (Mutable)** |
|------------|-----------------|-----------------------------|
| **Memory Usage** | More (New object every time) | Less (Modifies same object) |
| **Performance** | Slower | Faster |
| **Thread-Safe** | Yes | No (`StringBuilder`), Yes (`StringBuffer`) |

### **Example of Mutable String using StringBuilder**
```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello");
sb.append(" World"); 
System.out.println(sb); // Output: Hello World
```
✅ **Use `StringBuilder` for better performance!**

---

## **String vs StringBuffer vs StringBuilder**
| **Feature** | **String** | **StringBuffer** | **StringBuilder** |
|------------|------------|----------------|----------------|
| **Mutable?** | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| **Thread-Safe?** | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ❌ No |
| **Performance** | Slow | Slow | Fast |

---

## **String Tokenization (`split()` method)**
```java
String str = "apple,banana,grapes";
String[] fruits = str.split(",");

for (String fruit : fruits) {
    System.out.println(fruit);
}
```
**Output:**
```
apple
banana
grapes
```

